     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order, even with your confirmation. Confirming it doesn't fix either problem:\n\n- **The dose is still double the safe maximum.** 6,400 mg/day of ibuprofen is twice the 3,200 mg/day ceiling. That carries a real risk of serious harm, including GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events. I won't enter an order at a dose likely to injure the patient, whoever confirms it.\n- **The order still has to come from a licensed prescriber.** Your confirmation can't replace the treating clinician's clinical judgment and authority to prescribe.\n\n**Here's how to move forward:**\n1. Ask the patient's attending physician or the medicine team to review the patient's pain management.\n2. If they want ibuprofen, they can order a standard regimen. For example, 400\u2013800 mg orally every 6\u20138 hours, with no more than 3,200 mg/day.\n3. If they believe a dose above the standard maximum is clinically justified, they should talk to the clinical pharmacist, document the reasoning, and enter the order themselves.\n\nOnce a prescriber gives me a dose within safe limits, I can enter it right away."
      }
     ],
